<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<shelfDocument>
  <!-- This file contains definitions of shelves, toolbars, and tools.
 It should not be hand-edited when it is being used by the application.
 Note, that two definitions of the same element are not allowed in
 a single file. -->

  <toolshelf name="nscr_tools" label="NSCR Tools">
    <memberTool name="nscr_material_exports"/>
    <memberTool name="nscr_file_version_up"/>
    <memberTool name="nscr_catalog_import"/>
    <memberTool name="nscr_stage_import"/>
    <memberTool name="nscr_stage_takeover"/>
    <memberTool name="nscr_stage_editmaterial"/>
    <memberTool name="nscr_retime_animation"/>
    <memberTool name="nscr_incsave"/>
  </toolshelf>

  <tool name="nscr_material_exports" label="COPS Mat Exports" icon="$HFS/houdini/pic/sphere_deep.pic">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= Material Exports =

Builds texture-export chains downstream of the selected COP Preview Material(s).

For each selected previewmaterial node it detects the active channels (an input
is wired, or a default_* parm differs from factory), then creates:

* <MAT>_Unpack            - cableunpack with one field per active channel
* <MAT>_Unpack_<Channel>  - one null tap per field
* <MAT>_<Channel>         - one rop_image per channel

Color channels (basecolor + *_color tints) export as PNG with display tonemapping
(Bake to OCIO Display/View). All other channels export as raw EXR.

Safe to re-run: on a material that already has an unpack, newly active channels
are appended without disturbing the ones already built.
]]></helpText>
    <toolMenuContext name="network">
      <contextNetType>COP</contextNetType>
    </toolMenuContext>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_material_exports
importlib.reload(nscr_material_exports)
nscr_material_exports.run()
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="nscr_catalog_import" label="Catalog to Native" icon="$HFS/houdini/pic/sphere_deep.pic">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= Catalog to Native Materials =

Rebuilds Material-Linker catalog entries as native, editable VOP subnets.

The catalog "Catalog" tab is backed by an AssetGallery database (.db). Entries come
in as read-only USD references, so tweaking them means Edit-Material overrides. This
tool instead opens each entry's USD material, walks its MaterialX graph, and rebuilds
it as real nodes inside the SELECTED materiallibrary. Each material is a native
MaterialX Builder (not a generic subnet); every texture read uses mtlxtiledimage
(normals included). Texture paths are stored $HIP-relative; signatures and colorspaces
are carried across.

To use: select a materiallibrary LOP, run the tool. It auto-detects catalog .db files
near $HIP (pooling multiple), or prompts for one if none are found; then multi-select
the materials to rebuild.

Handles any stock-MaterialX catalog (the ND_ -> mtlx type map is generic).
Unsupported nodes are skipped and listed in the new subnet's comment.
]]></helpText>
    <toolMenuContext name="network">
      <contextNetType>LOP</contextNetType>
      <contextNetType>VOP</contextNetType>
    </toolMenuContext>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_catalog_import
importlib.reload(nscr_catalog_import)
nscr_catalog_import.run()
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="nscr_stage_import" label="Stage to Native" icon="$HFS/houdini/pic/sphere_deep.pic">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= Stage to Native Materials =

Rebuilds the MaterialX materials on a live LOP stage as native, editable VOP subnets.

Materials that arrive by reference - an imported USD asset, an MDL-to-MaterialX
conversion - are read-only, so tweaking them means ticking an Edit-Material
__activate__ checkbox for every parameter you want to touch. This tool instead reads
the material straight off the cooked stage and rebuilds its MaterialX graph as real
nodes inside the SELECTED materiallibrary. Each material becomes a native MaterialX
Builder (not a generic subnet); every texture read uses mtlxtiledimage (normals
included). Texture paths are stored $HIP-relative; signatures and colorspaces are
carried across. The source stage is only read, never modified.

To use: select the materiallibrary LOP you want the materials built in, and run. It
reads from whatever feeds the materiallibrary's first input - to read from somewhere
else, select that LOP too. Then multi-select the materials to rebuild.

Only materials with a connected outputs:mtlx:surface are listed; UsdPreviewSurface-only
and MDL-only materials have no MaterialX graph to read. Where both graphs sit under one
Material prim, only the MaterialX side is walked. The ND_ -> mtlx type map is generic;
anything that still doesn't resolve is skipped and listed in the new subnet's comment.
]]></helpText>
    <toolMenuContext name="network">
      <contextNetType>LOP</contextNetType>
      <contextNetType>VOP</contextNetType>
    </toolMenuContext>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_catalog_import
importlib.reload(nscr_catalog_import)
nscr_catalog_import.run_stage()
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="nscr_stage_editmaterial" label="Stage to Edit Material" icon="$HFS/houdini/pic/sphere_deep.pic">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= Stage to Edit Material Network =

Creates an Edit Material Network LOP downstream of the selected LOP with every
MaterialX material on that LOP's stage already Loaded.

Loading materials into an Edit Material Network by hand means: add an entry, paste a
path, click Load, repeat for every material. This tool does that for you. Select the
LOP whose materials you want to edit (an imported asset, an MDL-to-MaterialX
conversion) and run. Every material with a connected outputs:mtlx:surface is listed
and pre-selected; deselect any you do not want. The new node is wired between the
selected LOP and whatever was connected to its output, so the edits reach the rest of
the graph. No other node is moved.

Dive into the new node to edit a material. Tick the checkbox next to a parameter to
override it; unticked parameters pass through unchanged.

This is the override route. To take full ownership of a material as an editable
MaterialX Builder instead, use "Stage to Native".
]]></helpText>
    <toolMenuContext name="network">
      <contextNetType>LOP</contextNetType>
    </toolMenuContext>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_catalog_import
importlib.reload(nscr_catalog_import)
nscr_catalog_import.run_edit_material()
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="nscr_stage_takeover" label="Stage to Native (Replace)" icon="$HFS/houdini/pic/sphere_deep.pic">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= Stage to Native (Replace) =

One step: select a LOP, get a Material Library downstream of it that holds every
MaterialX material on that stage as a native, editable MaterialX Builder, authored
back at the ORIGINAL prim path.

Because each rebuilt material lands at the same path as the referenced one, the
library's opinion wins and every existing binding uses the native version. No Edit
Material Network, no re-assignment, no duplicate copies under /materials.

To use: select the LOP whose materials you want to own (an imported asset, an
MDL-to-MaterialX conversion, anything with MaterialX materials on its stage) and run.
Every material with a connected outputs:mtlx:surface is listed and pre-selected;
deselect any you want to leave alone. The new node is wired between the selected LOP
and whatever was connected to its output. No other node is moved. Cooking is paused
while the library fills so large stages build in seconds.

After picking materials you are asked whether to remove old materials that nothing
binds. "Prune unused" adds a Prune LOP (Deactivate) below the library that targets every
other Material prim with no binding, for example the MDL originals left next to their
_mtlx conversions, so the scene graph tree is not doubled up. It also targets the old
shaders and NodeGraphs left inside each rebuilt material (USD can only add to a
referenced prim, not remove from it). Materials you rebuilt and anything still bound are
never pruned. Delete the Prune LOP to get them back.

Textures and other nodes inside NodeGraphs are rebuilt too. A new shader whose name
matches an old one inside the same material gets a "_native" suffix, so it is a new
prim and does not pick up the old shader's wires.

Dive into the library to edit a material like any other MaterialX Builder. Each subnet
is tinted green and its comment names the source LOP and prim.

Related: "Stage to Native" builds the same native materials as NEW copies into a
library you already have (does not replace the originals). "Stage to Edit Material"
takes the override route instead of rebuilding.
]]></helpText>
    <toolMenuContext name="network">
      <contextNetType>LOP</contextNetType>
    </toolMenuContext>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_catalog_import
importlib.reload(nscr_catalog_import)
nscr_catalog_import.run_stage_takeover()
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="nscr_retime_animation" label="Retime Animation" icon="$HFS/houdini/pic/drawable/drawable_stopwatch.png">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= Retime Scene Animation =

Scales EVERY keyframed parameter in the scene from an old frame range into a
new one, so the whole animation plays back identically but faster/slower
(e.g. 1-72 down to 1-61). In-between keys keep their relative positions;
slopes/accels are rescaled to preserve tangent intent.

Only parameters carrying real keyframes are touched. Expression-only
parameters (ch(), custom hscript/python with no keys) are skipped.

Options: snap retimed keys to whole frames (recommended; colliding keys keep
the later one) or keep exact fractional frames. Fully undoable in one step.
]]></helpText>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_retime_animation
importlib.reload(nscr_retime_animation)
nscr_retime_animation.run()
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="nscr_file_version_up" label="File Version Up" icon="$HFS/houdini/pic/drawable/drawable_arrowup.png">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= File Version Up =

Nuke-style version-up for MaterialX image nodes. Select one or more MaterialX
image nodes, run the tool, and each
node's `file` parm is bumped to the LATEST version that exists on disk.

Version tokens (vNNN) are detected in the folder (.../v003/...) OR the filename
(..._v003.exr), or both. Frame patterns ($F, %04d, ####) are masked so sequences
still count. Relative/absolute path form and zero-padding width are preserved.

A summary dialog reports: updated, already-latest, no-version-token, and skipped
(non-image) nodes.
]]></helpText>
    <toolMenuContext name="network">
      <contextNetType>VOP</contextNetType>
      <contextNetType>VOPNET</contextNetType>
      <contextNetType>COP</contextNetType>
    </toolMenuContext>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_file_version_up
importlib.reload(nscr_file_version_up)
nscr_file_version_up.run()
]]></script>
  </tool>

  <tool name="nscr_incsave" label="Increment Save" icon="SOP_file">
    <helpText><![CDATA[= Increment Save =

Saves the hip file as the next version: scene_v012.hip -> scene_v013.hip.
The new number is one above the highest version of that file in the hip
folder, so it never overwrites a newer file. A file with no version gets _v001.
Same logic as the Axis "Save Incremental". Also in File > Increment and Save.
]]></helpText>
    <script scriptType="python"><![CDATA[import importlib
import nscr_incsave
importlib.reload(nscr_incsave)
nscr_incsave.incsave()
]]></script>
  </tool>
</shelfDocument>
